window.docContents[232].resolve({"/ISO-80000-7-___-Light-and-radiation/Quantity-kinds-and-units-of-ISO-80000-7/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--ISO-80000-7-___-Light-and-radiation--Quantity-kinds-and-units-of-ISO-80000-7":{"contents":"\n\nA catalogued kind pairs a dimensioned quantity-kind with its\nexact source in the series — the part, the printed item designation (e.g. \"7-6.1\"), the\nprincipal quantity symbol, and the coherent SI unit symbol. The citation travels with the\nkind as data, so the source of each definition can be rendered or audited downstream.\n\nRealized as structure CataloguedKind over a DimensionedKind, with cite rendering\ne.g. ISO 80000-7, Second edition, 2019-08 item 7-6.1. The catalogue lists all 66\nitems in item order — the speed of light (7-1.1), radiant flux (7-4.1), luminous\nintensity (7-14), the photon quantities (7-19.1 … 7-25), the molar absorption coefficient\n(7-37), … — each paired with its coherent SI unit symbol.\n\nThe candela reduces to power (item 7-14). Luminous intensity carries the dimension of\npower, M·L²·T⁻³ — the scale-spanning reduction (R13) made a checked computation: PhysLib\nhas no luminous-intensity generator, and none is needed, the spectral luminous efficiency\nV(λ) that relates the candela to the watt being dimensionless. Uses\nthe dimension map and the scale-spanning\nunits.\n\nluminousIntensity_dim_eq_power : luminousIntensity.dim = Dim.power, by reflexivity over\nDim.luminousIntensity := Dim.power. The companions radiantFlux_dim_{mass,length,time}\nrecord M·L²·T⁻³ for radiant flux, radiantEnergy_dim_time = -2 the joule,\nphotonFlux_dim_time = -1 the photon count rate, and\nmolarAbsorptionCoefficient_dim_length = 2 the molar coefficient as an area (the mole\nreduced).\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009ISO 80000-7 — Light and radiation","header":"26.1. Quantity-kinds and units of ISO 80000-7","id":"/ISO-80000-7-___-Light-and-radiation/Quantity-kinds-and-units-of-ISO-80000-7/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--ISO-80000-7-___-Light-and-radiation--Quantity-kinds-and-units-of-ISO-80000-7"},"/Standards-Reference-___-PKC-beside-ISO-80000-1-and-the-VIM/Units-and-the-individual-quantity/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Standards-Reference-___-PKC-beside-ISO-80000-1-and-the-VIM--Units-and-the-individual-quantity":{"contents":"* This work* Dybkær (2009)* ISO 80000-1:2022* VIM 3 (JCGM 200:2012)* VIM 4 2CD* MetrologicalUnit* §18.12* 6.2 unit* 1.9 measurement unit* 1.12 measurement unit* MetrologicalUnit.Commensurable* §9.13.4* 6.2 (of the same kind)* 1.24 conversion factor between units* 1.22 conversion factor between units* MetrologicalUnit.measure* §13.3.3* 6.2 (Q = {Q} [Q])* 1.19 quantity value; 1.20 Note 2* 1.24 value of a quantity; 1.25 Note 1* IndividualQuantity* Ch. 3* 4.1 individual quantity* 1.1 Note 1 (individual quantities)* 1.1 Notes 1–2 individual quantity (no entry)\n\n* Agreement. A unit belongs to one kind, a quantity of that kind is expressed as a\nnumber times the unit, and only quantities above the ordinal level have units.\nMetrologicalUnit.WellFormed requires an interval or ratio kind, which agrees with\nVIM 3 1.26 Note 1 and VIM 4 2CD 1.33 Note 4 that ordinal quantities have no units.* A MetrologicalUnit is not itself a quantity. VIM 3 defines the unit as a quantity\nadopted by convention, and VIM 4 2CD and ISO 80000-1:2022 6.2 as a reference quantity. A\nMetrologicalUnit is a record of the kind it references and its symbol. It is not a\nQuantity, and no magnitude is attached to it.* One unit, one kind. The withdrawn ISO 80000-1:2009 3.9 Note 2 observes that units of\nquantities of the\nsame dimension may share a name and symbol even when the quantities are not of the same\nkind. Here a unit names exactly one kind, so the joule of energy and a unit of moment\nof force with the same symbol are two units.* Commensurable holds when two units reference the same kind. It is the condition\nunder which the sources define a conversion factor between units; the factor itself is\nnot part of the relation. It is proved an equivalence.* measure forms the number-and-unit value, the product ISO 80000-1:2022 6.2 writes\nfor an individual quantity, its numerical value and its unit. Its number is an Int,\nas for PropertyValue.* IndividualQuantity o k R makes the bearer explicit. The sources distinguish the\nindividual quantity from the general one in a note and rely on context to tell them\napart. Here the bearer o is a parameter of the type, so quantities of two different\nobjects cannot be combined by mistake. The Lean source cites Dybkær's Chapter 3 for the\nobject.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Standards Reference — PKC beside ISO 80000-1 and the VIM","header":"34.7. Units and the individual quantity","id":"/Standards-Reference-___-PKC-beside-ISO-80000-1-and-the-VIM/Units-and-the-individual-quantity/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Standards-Reference-___-PKC-beside-ISO-80000-1-and-the-VIM--Units-and-the-individual-quantity"},"/Write-Once___-Correctly/The-five-steps/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Write-Once___-Correctly--The-five-steps--Step-2-___-Pair-each-kind-with-its-dimension":{"contents":"Give each kind its dimension, and let dimension do only what it can. The dimension\nmap is a forgetful functor — it keeps a kind's SI base-quantity exponents and discards the rest —\nso it answers only the coarse question, are these even commensurable? It\nrespects products, which is ordinary dimensional bookkeeping proved\nas a law, but it does not decide identity. This step matters precisely because the quantities\nthat carry the retrieval are almost all dimension one — a volume fraction, a reflectivity, an\nindex. Dimension equates them all; the kind layer keeps them apart. Pairing a kind with its\ndimension here is what lets a later product be checked for dimensional consistency and kind\nconsistency at once.In the example, volumetric and gravimetric water content (vwc, gwc) are both dimension one yet\ndistinct kinds — a units library alone would silently accept one for the other, which is exactly\nthe failure this layer removes. (The literal dim-functor collapse is the Mathlib-backed dimension\nlayer, shown in the Units and Dimension chapters; here the point is made one level up, at the\nkind.)\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Write Once, Correctly\u0009The five steps","header":"18.1.2. Step 2 — Pair each kind with its dimension","id":"/Write-Once___-Correctly/The-five-steps/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Write-Once___-Correctly--The-five-steps--Step-2-___-Pair-each-kind-with-its-dimension"}});